+        ///
+        /// On Linux, for stream (e.g. TCP) sockets, this flag makes the
+        /// kernel discard the received bytes from the socket without
+        /// copying *any* of them into the caller's buffer, no matter how
+        /// large the buffer is. The returned byte count still reflects how
+        /// many bytes were consumed from the stream, so it can look
+        /// exactly like a normal successful read. Callers that reuse the
+        /// same buffer across multiple [`recvmsg`] calls must not assume
+        /// that a nonzero byte count means the buffer was written to; when
+        /// this flag is set on a stream socket, the buffer keeps whatever
+        /// contents (e.g. leftover data from a previous call) it had
+        /// before the call.
+        ///
+        /// [`recvmsg`]: crate::net::recvmsg
         // Apple, illumos, and NetBSD have `MSG_TRUNC` but it's not documented
         // for use with `recv` and friends, and in practice appears to be
         // ignored.
